Now consider if a CPU has 4 cores, it executes only 4 tasks at a time by each thread.

if we provide 8 tasks then the thread running on each core saves the state of the current thread and execute another thread and then saves again and continue another. This is called as context switching.

Cons of manually creating a thread

1. Time consuming - as context switching takes time , cpu busy in context switching rather than executing task.
2. Thread Creation Time - it takes time to create the thread, if multiple threads are there, it will take more time.
3. Not Terminated Threads - If the threads are not terminated, it consumes memory in the system.

CPU thrashing -- maximum time CPU busy in context switching rather than executing threads.

+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         >>>  EXECUTOR FRAMEWORK  <<<                               |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Executor Framework is a concurrency framework provided by java that decouples the task submission and thread management. The thread creation and management will be handled by this framework. It provides reusable thread.

Earlier if we manually create 1000 threads, context switching takes more time and memory.

Now using executor framework, we give fixed number of threads needs to be created to framework and context switching happens between those threads which reduces CPU thrashing.

We can't completely eliminate the context switching.


Executor framework provide us the pool of thread and a queue. The pool contains the threads which executes the task concurrently and queue contains the pending tasks if no threads available in the pool


