|             THREAD POOL EXECUTOR         |
+------------------------------------------+


ThreadPoolExecutor is a class that gives us fine-grained control over how a thread pool is configured. If we need a standard/common thread-pool configuration, we can use the Executors factory methods.

ThreadPoolExecutor in constructor takes 5 values - int corePoolSize, int maxPoolSize, long keepAliveTime, Timeunit unit, BlockingQueue workqueue.

corePoolSize - default number of threads in the pool.
maxPoolSize - maximum number of threads in the pool.
keepTimeAlive - how how much time the helper thread will remains in the pool.
unit - unit of time for helper threads like SECONDS,MINUTES etc.
BlockingQueue - queue to maintain the pending tasks.

if corePoolSize=2 and maxPoolsize=5, then 2 threads are core threads and 3 threads are helper threads. These helper threads are created when the queue is full and core threads are already executing some tasks.

example - we have corePoolSize as 2, maxPoolSize as 5, queue size is 2, we have 5 tasks coming up immediately (1,2,3,4,5). 1 and 2 has been executed by core thread.
3 and 4 has been added to the queue, now 5th task cominng up. pool is busy, queue is not empty --> new helper thread created, which takes task 5, completed that task,
helper thread comes to pool. wait for keep time alive and has been killed if no other task.

